{{/*
  Shared type-mechanics partials (E-1822).

  These are the handoff lines whose drift between the spawn rendering and the
  claim-into-a-live-session rendering would be a bug: which worktree the session
  is bound to and the `--db main` routing that implies, the one-session-one-task
  rule, and the per-type deliverable / terminal-status rule. The per-type
  wrappers (handoff/{todo,bugfix,epic,research,brainstorm}.md.tmpl) and the claim
  wrapper (handoff/claim.md.tmpl) both pull from here, so a change lands in both.

  Everything else stays in the wrappers — arrival framing is exactly what differs
  between "spawned for this task" and "claimed it mid-session", so it is NOT
  shared. Do not pre-factor further; extract a line only when a second consumer
  needs it.

  Branching is on `.task_type` (the *effective* type — the Python spawn path
  normalizes an unknown/absent type to `todo` before rendering) rather than
  forking into five near-identical partials.
*/ -}}

{{define "handoff_worktree" -}}
Your worktree is: {{.worktree_path}} (branch {{.branch}}) — confirm with `pwd`. Don't edit the main checkout. The worktree routes DB access to a sandbox, so every `endless` command that must reach the main database takes `--db main`.
{{- end}}

{{/*
  E-2120 rewrote the non-brainstorm discovery branch. The old bullet 1 asked
  "could it reasonably be done now, INSIDE THE WORK ALREADY UNDERWAY?" — a
  topical-membership question, and sessions answered it honestly: a stray id in
  a guide paragraph is not part of `verb update`'s subject, so they fell through
  to "otherwise file it". That is the wrong axis, and it had been given as the
  justification for over-filing more than once. The axis is what the fix costs
  to make and to review; the real bound the guide protects is a LARGE unrelated
  change, which is a size test, not a kinship test.

  Bullet 1 also carries a third obligation now: record the grown scope on the
  task. The commit message and the reply are both transient — the commit is
  found only by someone already reading that diff, the reply dies with the
  session — so a task whose plan describes only its original scope is a lie
  about what shipped under it.

  And filing is no longer a branch the session takes alone. A session that files
  reflexively inflates the backlog; one that drops a finding silently loses it.
  It has the context to explain a finding and not the standing to rule on
  whether it earns a row, so the terminal branch is a QUESTION with a fixed
  shape (what, why it matters to someone who was not here, for, against,
  recommendation) and the user's answer decides.

  Two clauses come back from docs/guide/tasks.md, which this partial compressed
  away when it folded the guide's numbered tests into bullets. Both are
  load-bearing at the decision point, and a session that never opens the guide
  met neither:

    - The FRAMING. ED-1550 makes filing arithmetic — an agent that files more
      than it closes gives the project no completion date — so the list has to
      open by saying filing is the exception. Without it the bullets read as a
      menu of equals.

    - The OVERRIDE, which outranks the size bound. `handoff_close` orders every
      session to run the project-wide regression and report it, and until now
      nothing told it what to do when that suite comes back red for a reason
      that is not its own. It cannot report the suite green and it cannot leave
      it red, so the fix is its to make whatever the failure belongs to. This
      is the live case the whole task came from: E-2114's regression turned up
      a one-sentence defect in a file already open, and the session filed it.

      Phrased around the obligation to RUN AND REPORT, not around being
      blocked. The guide's old condition ("a drive-by you genuinely cannot
      complete the task without") did not match its own example ("a test that
      fails for an unrelated reason") — an unrelated red test rarely stops you
      finishing — so a session found the example matching, the condition not
      matching, and fell through to filing.
*/ -}}
{{define "handoff_focus" -}}
Stay focused on E-{{.spawned_id}} — one session, one task. {{if eq .task_type "brainstorm"}}As ideas resolve into concrete work, file them as new tasks (`--cleans-up E-{{.spawned_id}}`) or decisions (`endless decision add`) rather than building inline.
   Filing more than one? Check whether they share a root cause — file the
   cause, not each symptom.{{else}}Filing is the exception, not the default. For anything you discover along the way:
   - Could it reasonably be done now? Do it — note it in the commit message,
     record the grown scope on the task (`endless task update E-{{.spawned_id}}
     --plan-file <path> --keep-status --db main`), and say so in your reply
     draft. The test is COST and reviewer confusion, not kinship: "not a pure
     example of this task" is not a reason to file, and a fix cheaper to make
     than the task row describing it gets folded in. The bound is size — a
     large or risky unrelated change still splits out, because it inflates the
     diff you are reviewed on, couples two changes into one land, and widens a
     revert's blast radius.
   - Is it a bug in work THIS session landed? Reopen that task
     (`endless task update E-NNN --status revisit --db main`) and fix it there.
   - Otherwise ASK me before filing — that call is mine, not yours. Give me
     what you saw and why it would matter to someone who was not in this
     session, the case for filing, the case against, and your recommendation.
     File or drop it on my answer.
   - Filing on that answer: `--cleans-up E-{{.spawned_id}}`, and confirm before
     implementing.
   - Filing more than one? Check whether they share a root cause — file the
     cause, not each symptom.
   - A check you are obliged to run and report — the project-wide regression —
     outranks all of this. Fix the minimum that clears a red test or a broken
     build even when the cause is out of scope, and say so explicitly. You
     cannot report that suite green and you cannot leave it red.{{end}}
{{- end}}

{{/*
  E-1947: the ask-first rule for the two destructive worktree verbs, plus what
  to do INSTEAD of a drop. Extracted because all seven wrappers already carried
  the same sentence verbatim, and the guidance half of E-1947 has to reach every
  one of them: a gate stops the damage but not the advice, and twice recently a
  session RECOMMENDED dropping a worktree whose history had merely diverged.
  Emits one paragraph with no leading marker, so it drops into a numbered item,
  a bullet, or mid-sentence unchanged.

  E-2073 split the two verbs. One sentence governing both put a precondition —
  have I been asked? — in front of a destructive act, and the asking is where it
  failed: on 2026-08-25 two sessions ten minutes apart destroyed worktrees, and
  neither OVERRODE the rule. Both concluded conversational text had satisfied
  the precondition. One caused real damage. `land` keeps "without asking"
  because it is the normal end of a task and the spawning session owns the
  timing. Removal gets NEVER, with nothing left to evaluate.

  It names four commands because it has to name the OUTCOME: `drop`, `reap`,
  `git worktree remove` and `rm -r` all reach the same destroyed worktree, so a
  drop-only prohibition is honoured by reaching for the next route down.

  E-2073 also stopped relying on the prose alone. blockWorktreeRemovalIfApplicable
  in internal/hookcmd/claude.go refuses all four routes at the tool layer, with no
  bypass, so the paragraph now describes a gate rather than asking for restraint.
  That is why it no longer says worktree lifecycle belongs to the SPAWNING
  session: the hook fires on any Claude session's Bash tool, so no agent removes
  a worktree, and the person running the session does it in their own shell.
*/ -}}
{{define "handoff_land_drop" -}}
Don't run `endless worktree land` without asking. NEVER remove a worktree, and never OFFER to — not `endless worktree drop`, not `endless worktree reap`, not `git worktree remove`, not `rm -r` on the directory. A PreToolUse hook refuses all four, so this is not a rule you can decide has been waived; removal is not an agent's to perform, and the person running this session does it themselves if it ever comes to that. Retention is the design. If a branch's history has diverged from main, the fix is `git rebase main` or `git reset --hard main` **in place**; removal deletes the directory a live session is sitting in, and is only for abandoning the work itself. If removal looks warranted, say so once and stop.
{{- end}}

{{define "handoff_deliverable" -}}
{{if eq .task_type "research"}}Findings are the deliverable. Write them into `tasks.outcome` via `endless task update E-{{.spawned_id}} --outcome-file <path> --db main` (or `--outcome '<inline text>'`). End state is `unreviewed` — not `unverified` (research never enters that lane, ED-1502) and not `completed`, which is your user's call after they read the findings (E-2016).

{{else if eq .task_type "brainstorm"}}The synthesis is the deliverable. Write it into `tasks.outcome` via `endless task update E-{{.spawned_id}} --outcome-file <path> --db main` (or `--outcome '<inline text>'`), and spawn the decisions/tasks it produces (`endless decision add ...`, `endless task add ... --cleans-up E-{{.spawned_id}}`). End state is `unreviewed --outcome` — a brainstorm produces information, not testable behavior, so it never enters the `unverified` lane; and `completed` is your user's call after they read the synthesis (E-2016).

{{else if eq .task_type "epic"}}You're the coordinator on this epic. Don't implement directly — draft plans, tell the user which children to spawn, verify their work.

{{end}}
{{- end}}

{{define "handoff_terminal" -}}
{{if eq .task_type "research"}}When findings are ready: `endless task update E-{{.spawned_id}} --status unreviewed --outcome-file <path> --db main`, then tell me where to read them. Don't mark `completed` yourself — that is mine to set once I have read them — and don't mark `confirmed`/`assumed`, which research never uses.
{{- else if eq .task_type "brainstorm"}}When the synthesis is ready: `endless task update E-{{.spawned_id}} --status unreviewed --outcome-file <path> --db main`, then tell me where to read it and which follow-ups it spawned. Don't mark `completed` yourself — that is mine to set once I have read it — and don't mark `confirmed`/`assumed`, which a brainstorm never uses.
{{- else if eq .task_type "epic"}}When all children are `confirmed`/`assumed`: `endless task update E-{{.spawned_id}} --status completed --db main`, then tell me. Don't mark the epic complete while children are still open.
{{- else}}When implementation is done: `endless task update E-{{.spawned_id}} --status unverified --db main`, then hand me the one verify command. Don't mark `confirmed`/`assumed` yourself.
{{- end}}
{{- end}}

{{define "handoff_mechanics" -}}
{{template "handoff_worktree" .}}

{{template "handoff_focus" .}}

{{template "handoff_deliverable" .}}{{template "handoff_terminal" .}}
{{- end}}
